; tick_count.pe — counts timer ticks through the STATUS flag.
;
; This program exists to test ONE thing: that a tick is never lost when the
; firmware's STATUS read lands on the same cycle as the counter wrap.
;
; uart_echo.pe deliberately does not use STATUS -- it polls the TIMER VALUE and
; waits for it to change, because a read-and-clear flag cannot be polled in a
; loop that also needs to see it. That left port 7 with no firmware exercising
; it at all, and the defect sat there through a 16/16 green regression:
; `tick_flag` was driven by two always_ff blocks, which Icarus raced (losing
; ~17% of ticks) and yosys resolved to a CONSTANT 0, so STATUS worked in
; simulation and was dead in silicon.
;
; The loop below is the adversarial case on purpose. It is two instructions
; long when idle, so `IN A, STATUS` asserts io_re every other cycle and
; collides with the tick counter's wrap as often as this SoC can arrange.
;
; Contract under test: after N ticks of real time, the count assembled here
; equals the free-running TIMER, because every tick is reported exactly once.
;
;   dmem[0] = ticks observed via STATUS
;
; Both counters are 8-bit and wrap together, so the testbench must compare
; them over a window shorter than 256 ticks.

        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A                 ; count := 0

poll:
        IN    A, STATUS            ; read-and-clear: a set flag is consumed here
        JZ    poll                 ; no tick yet -- 2-cycle loop, io_re every 2nd

        ; A tick was reported. Bank it.
        LDM   A, 0
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   0, A
        JMP   poll
